  "content": {
    "id": "p29-q2-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "2",
    "prompt": "$$(1+x)^n={}^nC_0+{}^nC_1x+\\cdots+{}^nC_nx^n\\qquad(*)$$\nFor $n\\ge1$. Use it to prove the following results and interpret the result in terms of a general row $n$ in Pascal’s triangle.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p29-q2-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "By substituting $x=1$, show that ${}^nC_0+{}^nC_1+\\cdots+{}^nC_n=2^n$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Substitute $x=1$ into $(*)$: ${}^nC_0+{}^nC_1+\\cdots+{}^nC_n=(1+1)^n=2^n$. The sum of row $n$ is $2^n$.",
          "worked": "Substitute $x=1$ into $(*)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(1+1)^n&={}^nC_0+{}^nC_1+\\cdots+{}^nC_n\\\\2^n&={}^nC_0+{}^nC_1+\\cdots+{}^nC_n.\\end{align*}$$\nThe entries in row $n$ of Pascal’s triangle sum to $2^n$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q2-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "By substituting $x=-1$, show that\n$${}^nC_0+{}^nC_2+{}^nC_4+\\cdots={}^nC_1+{}^nC_3+{}^nC_5+\\cdots.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Substituting $x=-1$ gives $0={}^nC_0-{}^nC_1+{}^nC_2-\\cdots$. Hence the entries with even lower indices and those with odd lower indices have equal sums.",
          "worked": "Substitute $x=-1$ into $(*)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(1-1)^n&={}^nC_0-{}^nC_1+{}^nC_2-{}^nC_3+\\cdots\\\\0&={}^nC_0-{}^nC_1+{}^nC_2-{}^nC_3+\\cdots.\\end{align*}$$\nRearranging gives\n$${}^nC_0+{}^nC_2+{}^nC_4+\\cdots={}^nC_1+{}^nC_3+{}^nC_5+\\cdots.$$\nIn row $n$ of Pascal’s triangle, the entries with even lower indices sum to the same total as those with odd lower indices.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q2-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence, by using the result of parts a and b, show that\n$${}^nC_0+{}^nC_2+{}^nC_4+\\cdots=2^{n-1}.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The two equal sums from part b have total $2^n$ by part a. Each is therefore $2^{n-1}$, half the row total.",
          "worked": "From part a,\n$${}^nC_0+{}^nC_1+\\cdots+{}^nC_n=2^n.$$\nFrom part b, the sums with even and odd lower indices are equal. Therefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}2\\left({}^nC_0+{}^nC_2+{}^nC_4+\\cdots\\right)&=2^n\\\\{}^nC_0+{}^nC_2+{}^nC_4+\\cdots&=2^{n-1}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe entries with even lower indices in row $n$ sum to half the total of the whole row.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 49
      }
    ]
  },
